<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.10.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01431</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.10.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>be9d9e1e-875e-4e7f-a6eb-f495d4c03b44</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section>
    <section class="check-understanding">
      <exercise data-content-id="c35a4cc7-b899-4f3e-96f6-3425cf6e20d2" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>A store receives 2,000 decks of popular trading cards. The number of decks of cards is a function, \(d\), of the number of days, \(t\)<emphasis>,</emphasis> since the shipment arrived. Here is a table showing some values of \(d\)<emphasis>.</emphasis>
          
          <table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
            <thead>
              <row>
                <entry><emphasis>\(t\)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(d(t)\)</emphasis></entry></row>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  0
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  2,000
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  5
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  1,283
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  10
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  823
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  15
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  528
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  20
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  338
                </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          
          Calculate the average rate of change for the following intervals:
          
          
        </para>
        <para>day 0 to day 5</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.10.2.  </para>
          <para>The correct answer is –143.4 decks per day. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise data-content-id="e8e769fe-91d9-4b6a-ba07-d1346e99b404" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>day 15 to day 20</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.10.2. </para>
          <para>The correct answer is –38 decks per day. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise data-content-id="8af1eca5-26aa-4e3e-a751-2fbe46bf3a21" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>A study was conducted to analyze the effects on deer population in a particular area. Let \(f\) be an exponential function that gives the population of deer \(t\) years after the study began.

          If \(f(t)=a \cdot b^t\) and the population is increasing, <emphasis>select three</emphasis> statements that must be true.</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.10.1 – 5.10.2.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is A, C, E.
      </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise data-content-id="f850d543-3061-41a4-9114-0324b7017a90" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>Function \(f\) models the population, in thousands, of a city \(t\) years after 1930.
          <figure><media alt="Graph of a function on a grid."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cc05abb75236376b7d1774698260940fb9b0c5b9"/></media></figure><para>The average rate of change of \(f\) from \(t=0\) to \(t=70\) is approximately 14 thousand people per year.
              Is this value a good way to describe the population change of the city over that time period? </para>
          </para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.10.3. </para>
          <para>The correct answer is no. Near 1930, the average rate of change was much less than 14 thousand people per year. Approaching the year 2000, the average rate of change was much greater than 14 thousand people per year.
      </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise data-content-id="0ebf5e2b-418d-4722-88e3-a4f74ac76eb9" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>The rate of change between years 1940 and 1950 is greater than/less than the rate of change between years 1960 and 1970. </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.10.3. </para>
          <para>The correct answer is less than. The rate of change increases as the time increases. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise data-content-id="afb5e7e2-0d1f-4b4e-831d-e2385f6acf77" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>The function, \(f\), gives the number of copies of a book that have sold \(w\) weeks after it was published. The equation \(f(w)=500 \cdot 2^w\) defines this function. <emphasis>Select two</emphasis> domains for which the average rate of change could be a good measure for the number of books sold. </para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.10.4.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is A, C. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise data-content-id="15f92bae-3f58-40c2-9e38-a85a6f0c7842" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para> The moth population over 10 weeks is graphed below. 
          <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b4f87d5892b899b11c44697eeb64c78983333dfd"/></media></figure><para>After 4 weeks, the population was 40 thousand. What was the average rate of change from the start to the 4th week? Do not enter commas in your answer.</para>
          </para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.10.3. 
      </para>
          <para>The correct answer is –115,000.
      </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise data-content-id="a311646d-b68d-4581-8f58-5b937c8bcf6c" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>The rate of change between weeks 1 and 2 is greater than/less than the rate of change between weeks 3 and 4. </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.10.3. </para>
          <para>The correct answer is greater than. The rate of change decreases as the time increases. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </section>
</content>
